🧠 ACTIVE DIRECTORY (AD) — QUICK REVISION NOTES
🏢 1. Windows Domain Basics
Active Directory (AD) = Central database that stores:
Users, passwords, emails, permissions
Domain Controller (DC) = Server that runs AD

👉 Purpose:

Manage all users & computers from one place
Avoid manual setup on every machine
✅ Advantages
Centralized user management
Centralized security policies (GPOs)
Same login works across all systems

👉 Example:

One login works on all university computers

🧱 2. AD Core: AD DS
Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) = Core service of AD
Stores all objects in the network:
Users, computers, groups, printers, etc.

👤 3. Objects in AD
🔹 Users
Security principals (can authenticate + get permissions)

Types:

Normal users → employees
Service accounts → run services (IIS, MSSQL)
💻 Machines (Computer Accounts)
Every domain computer = account
Naming format: COMPUTERNAME$

Key points:

Auto-generated password (~120 random chars)
Password rotates automatically
Acts as local admin on its own machine
👥 Security Groups
Used to assign permissions to multiple users

👉 Instead of giving access individually, assign to group

Important Groups:
Domain Admins → full control
Server Operators → manage DCs
Backup Operators → access all files
Account Operators → manage accounts
Domain Users → all users
Domain Computers → all machines

🗂️ 4. Organizational Units (OUs)
Containers used to organize users/computers
Usually based on departments (IT, HR, Sales)

👉 Purpose:

Apply policies (GPOs)

⚠️ Rules:

User → only in ONE OU
User → can be in MULTIPLE groups
🔥 OU vs Groups
Feature	OU	Group
Purpose	Apply policies	Give permissions
Membership	One OU	Multiple groups
Use case	Departments	Resource access

🛠️ 5. Managing AD
Tool:
Active Directory Users & Computers

You can:

Create/delete users
Reset passwords
Organize OUs
Default Containers:
Builtin → default groups
Users → default users
Computers → new machines
Domain Controllers → DCs
Managed Service Accounts → service accounts

🔑 6. Delegation
Giving limited control to users

👉 Example:

IT support can reset passwords

Key idea:

Avoid giving full admin access
Give only required permissions

💻 7. Managing Computers

Best practice:

Separate OUs for:
Workstations
Servers

📜 8. Group Policy (GPO)
Used to apply configurations to:
Users
Computers

Examples:

Disable control panel
Enforce password rules

Stored in:

SYSVOL share

🔐 9. Authentication
🟢 Kerberos (Default)

Uses ticket-based system:

User logs in → gets TGT
Uses TGT → requests TGS
Uses TGS → accesses service

Key points:

No repeated password sending
TGT encrypted with krbtgt hash
Uses session keys
🔴 NetNTLM (Legacy)

Uses challenge-response:

Server sends challenge
Client responds using password hash
DC verifies

Key points:

Password not sent over network
Less secure than Kerberos

🌳 10. Trees, Forests, Trusts
🌳 Tree
Multiple domains with same namespace

Example:

thm.local
uk.thm.local
us.thm.local
🌲 Forest
Multiple trees with different namespaces

Example:

thm.local + mht.com
🔗 Trust Relationships

Allows users from one domain to access another

Types:
One-way trust
A trusts B → B users access A
Two-way trust
Both domains trust each other

Key point:

Trust does NOT mean full access
Permissions still controlled manually
⚔️ 11. How This Is Used in Pentesting / Red Team

This is where everything becomes powerful 👇

🔍 1. User & Group Enumeration
Find:
Domain Admins
Privileged groups
Goal → privilege escalation
🔑 2. Password Attacks
Target:
Weak passwords
Service accounts

Common attacks:

Password spraying
Credential stuffing
🎟️ 3. Kerberos Attacks
Kerberoasting → crack service tickets
Pass-the-Ticket → reuse stolen tickets
Golden Ticket → full domain control (using krbtgt hash)
⚡ 4. NetNTLM Attacks
Capture hashes (LLMNR/NBT-NS poisoning)
Relay attacks

👉 Easier to exploit than Kerberos

🧑‍💻 5. Delegation Abuse
Misconfigured delegation = privilege escalation
Can reset passwords or control accounts
🏢 6. GPO Abuse
Modify GPO → run malicious scripts
Gain control over many machines
💻 7. Machine Account Abuse
Extract machine credentials
Move laterally across network
🔗 8. Trust Exploitation
Abuse trust relationships
Move between domains (lateral movement)
🧠 9. Lateral Movement
Use stolen credentials/tickets
Access:
Other machines
Servers
Domain Controller
🎯 Main Red Team Goal in AD
Reach Domain Admin
Control entire domain
